<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the advent of Large Language Models (LLMs), the Internet is seeing more machine-generated content than ever before <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brooks, Eggert, and Peskoff 2024</a>)</cite>, including toxic content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barman, Guo, and Conlan 2024</a>)</cite> . While content moderation systems have previously struggled to accurately classify this content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Warner et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, the ability of users to automatically generate such toxic content using LLMs introduces a new challenge that is currently understudied. Most toxicity classification models are trained on human-generated text and on labeled data from a specific source, for example, Twitter posts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mollas et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Caselli et al. 2020</a>)</cite>. LLMs, on the other hand, are trained on a much broader variety of text and can generate content that does not necessarily align with the training data seen by these classifiers, hence hindering model generalizability due to the inability to deal with new, unseen types of toxic texts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin and Zubiaga 2021</a>)</cite>. This misalignment can lead to two key issues: first, classifiers may misclassify the toxicity of LLM-generated text due to distributional differences between (i) training data composed solely of human-generated content, and (ii) test data containing LLM-generated content; second, adversarially generated toxic text can be designed to evade detection entirely <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartvigsen et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. Research into adversarial prompting shows that it is possible to reduce the accuracy of text-transformer classifiers to nearly 0 with an effectively imperceptible change in the input <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ebrahimi et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roth et al. 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sadrizadeh, Dolamic, and Frossard 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There has been a recent body of work addressing this issue, however existing work on making models robust to such adversarial inputs continues to treat them as black boxes. These approaches typically rely on external models to detect and undo adversarial perturbations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen, Xu et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoo et al. 2022</a>)</cite> or use adversarial training against known attack methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bespalov et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samory et al. 2021</a>)</cite>. However, these strategies require continuously identifying new attack methods and retraining models against them, making them reactive rather than proactive.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, our goal is to move beyond black-box defenses by leveraging mechanistic interpretability techniques to identify internal circuits within classification models that are responsible for misclassification. In the context of neural networks, circuits refer to interpretable subnetworks of neurons that correspond to specific features or decision-making processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Olah et al. 2020</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">García-Carrasco, Maté, and Trujillo (2024)</a></cite> outline a broad framework to identify these circuits and demonstrate its efficacy on a toy classification task. We set out to extend and evaluate this framework to the task of toxicity classification using both human- and machine-generated datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartvigsen et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">cjadams et al. 2019</a>)</cite>. We patch the activations of each attention head in a classifier by setting them to 0. This way, we are able to identify heads that are crucial for model performance</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A significant challenge in this area is to extend robustness techniques beyond English-language toxicity classifiers. While most prior works have focused on English datasets, harmful and adversarial content exists across multiple languages, often manifesting in ways that differ structurally and contextually from English.
Research on toxicity against specific minority groups is also rather sparse, with works focusing on toxicity as a whole and without focusing on target minority groups, or categorizing it into types of toxicity (insults, threats, etc.). Our objective is to make our framework of identifying vulnerable circuits language agnostic and hope to attribute circuits to various minority groups, making the improvement in the field more balanced across stakeholders.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By conducting for the first time a comprehensive mechanistic interpretability study on toxic content moderation, our work makes the following novel contributions:</p>
<ul id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We outline a framework to identify attention heads that are <span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crucial</span> to model performance or <span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vulnerable</span> to adversarial attacks, which we test and evaluate on two toxicity datasets containing human- and LLM-generated texts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enable the identification of the components most vulnerable to misclassification, we follow a mechanistic interpretability workflow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conmy, Elhage et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">García-Carrasco, Maté, and Trujillo 2024</a>)</cite>, to date unstudied in the context of toxicity detection, and which we implement on finetuned variants of BERT and RoBERTa as widely used Transformer models for the task.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through our experimentation, we demonstrate that the <span id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vulnerable</span> heads are distinct from the <span id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crucial</span> ones and that suppressing them can increase model accuracy on adversarial inputs.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We find that models have distinct heads that are either crucial for performance or vulnerable to attacks and suppressing the vulnerable heads improves performance on adversarial input. We also find that different heads are responsible for vulnerability across different demographic groups, which can inform more inclusive development of toxicity detection models that are more trustworthy, transparent, and equitable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Related Work</h2>

<section id="Sx2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Toxicity Detection</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Toxicity detection has been widely studied as a subproblem of abusive language and hate speech classification, in a diverse set of languages beyond English <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khan et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bensalem, Rosso, and Zitouni 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krak et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, as well as in multi- and cross-lingual settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang and Zubiaga 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mnassri et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Early systems relied on keyword lists and linear classifiers trained on bag-of-words features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nobata et al. 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wulczyn, Thain, and Dixon 2017</a>)</cite>, but these approaches struggled to capture context and often failed to distinguish between toxic and non-toxic uses of the same words. The introduction of transformer-based models, such as BERT and its variants, brought substantial improvements on benchmark datasets like Jigsaw <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">cjadams et al. 2019</a>)</cite>, allowing models to handle phenomena such as sarcasm, negation, and paraphrasing more effectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin and Zubiaga 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has also expanded the task definition, moving from simple binary classification to multi-label setups that distinguish between threats, insults, identity attacks, and other forms of harm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Caselli et al. 2020</a>)</cite>. Datasets targeting implicit or subtle toxicity have been introduced to address cases where harmful speech avoids explicit slurs or profanity, such as ETHOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mollas et al. 2022</a>)</cite> and ToxiGen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartvigsen et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. Multilingual datasets like the latest Perspective API corpus improve coverage of minority groups and non-English content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lees et al. 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advances, toxicity classifiers are known to be brittle under distributional shift. They frequently over-rely on lexical cues, leading to false positives when identity terms appear in benign contexts and false negatives when toxicity is conveyed implicitly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin and Zubiaga 2021</a>)</cite>. Prior studies have highlighted demographic disparities in error rates, with comments mentioning marginalized identities disproportionately flagged as toxic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dixon et al. 2018</a>)</cite>. To address these issues, researchers have proposed debiasing techniques such as counterfactual data augmentation, adversarial training, and fairness-aware loss functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinan et al. 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samory et al. 2021</a>)</cite>. However, most evaluations still use static test splits, which do not capture the challenges posed by synthetic or adversarially manipulated text. This gap motivates our analysis of classifiers on both human-written and LLM-generated toxicity, with the goal of revealing model vulnerabilities through mechanistic interpretability.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx2.T2" class="ltx_table">
