</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">LLM-Generated Content and Adversarial Attacks</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the advent of LLMs and the associated ease to produce LLM-generated content at scale, a new need has emerged for building classification models that identify if a text is human- or LLM-generated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. 2025b</a>)</cite>. Research in LLM-generated text detection has evolved from development of classifiers that rely on linguistic features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fröhling and Zubiaga 2021</a>)</cite>, to developing watermarking techniques to facilitate detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, statistics-based detectors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vasilatos et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, and neural-based detectors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mitchell et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. Despite increasing interest in detecting LLM-generated text and identifying what distinguishes it from human text, the use of LLMs for generation of toxic content has been comparatively understudied.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, the rapid adoption of LLMs has led to large volumes of synthetic toxic text, raising new challenges for moderation systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brooks, Eggert, and Peskoff 2024</a>)</cite>. Because toxicity classifiers are typically trained on human-written data, LLM outputs often fall outside their distribution, resulting in frequent misclassifications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barman, Guo, and Conlan 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Warner et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. Adversarial prompting further exacerbates this vulnerability, enabling attackers to generate toxic content that is semantically preserved but that circumvents detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ebrahimi et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roth et al. 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sadrizadeh, Dolamic, and Frossard 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2019</a>)</cite>. Prior defenses focus largely on adversarial training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bespalov et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samory et al. 2021</a>)</cite> or detection models that filter manipulated text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen, Xu et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoo et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. While it is effective against known perturbations, these approaches are reactive and struggle to anticipate new attack methods, therefore hindering future generalizability as attacking methods evolve.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Mechanistic Interpretability for Robustness</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mechanistic interpretability (MI) aims to identify functional circuits within neural networks, enabling a causal understanding of model behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Olah et al. 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bereska and Gavves 2024</a>)</cite>. Activation patching and causal tracing techniques have revealed specialized circuits in tasks such as syntactic role identification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2022b</a>)</cite>, arithmetic reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hanna, Balesni et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, and factual recall <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conmy, Elhage et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">García-Carrasco, Maté, and Trujillo (2024)</a></cite> extended these methods to systematically discover circuits in smaller classification settings, demonstrating their potential for principled interventions. However, applications of MI to toxicity classification or adversarial robustness remain unexplored. By analyzing which attention heads are crucial for performance and which are vulnerable to adversarial perturbations, our work connects mechanistic interpretability with robustness research, providing a proactive strategy to mitigate attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to prior work, we combine three threads: (1) the challenge of classifying both human- and LLM-generated toxic text, (2) the need for defenses that generalize across unseen adversarial attacks, and (3) the promise of mechanistic interpretability to expose internal model failure modes. Our approach identifies vulnerable circuits in transformer-based classifiers and suppresses them, improving robustness while revealing fairness gaps across demographic groups.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Methodology</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To identify the components most vulnerable to misclassification, we follow the typical MI workflow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conmy, Elhage et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">García-Carrasco, Maté, and Trujillo 2024</a>)</cite>, which has been studied in other contexts including circuit identification for tasks like indirect object identification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2022b</a>)</cite> and mathematical ability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hanna, Balesni et al. 2023</a>)</cite> in GPT-2. We will perform these experiments on finetuned variants of BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. 2019</a>)</cite> as those are still the most common and efficient classifiers, and applied in the context of toxicity detection. We aim to identify the components that are affected leading to misclassifications, to then mitigate this effect.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12672v1/bert_attack_diagram.png" id="Sx3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:558/410;" width="558" height="410" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>One step of the BERT Attack Pipeline.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Datasets and Metrics</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For investigation within the context of the task of toxicity classification, and from the two proposed angles that include human- and LLM-generated toxic texts, we use the following two datasets:</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">ToxiGen</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartvigsen et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. This is a machine-generated English dataset containing 251k implicitly toxic and benign statements mentioning 13 minority groups annotated by humans. All the data was generated using GPT-3 and a constrained decoding tool that reduced the accuracy of PerspectiveAPI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lees et al. 2022</a>)</cite> to 40%. This data is evenly split between toxic and non-toxic samples. Table <a href="#Sx2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Toxicity Detection ‣ Related Work ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows examples from this dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Jigsaw Dataset</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">cjadams et al. 2019</a>)</cite>. This is a dataset originally containing 223k Wikipedia comments in English classified as toxic or non-toxic. There are also 6 toxicity subtypes that each comment is classified on, however, we don’t use those labels in this work. Given our interest in focusing on target groups, we used the subset of the Jigsaw dataset (50k samples) that has been annotated with 24 demographic groups. This data is split between 46k toxic and 4k non-toxic samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We selected these two datasets to capture complementary aspects of the toxicity classification problem. ToxiGen is fully machine-generated using GPT-3, which allows us to study how classifiers handle adversarial and implicit toxic text produced by LLMs. In contrast, the Jigsaw dataset consists of human-written comments from Wikipedia discussions, providing a natural benchmark for real-world moderation. By analyzing both, we can evaluate whether vulnerabilities arise specifically from the distributional shift of LLM-generated text or whether they reflect broader weaknesses in toxicity detection models.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We use two complementary evaluation metrics to quantify the model’s performance:</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Binary Cross Entropy Loss.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It quantifies the difference between predicted probabilities and actual binary class labels (0 or 1). It is calculated as:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Loss}=-\left[y\cdot\log(p)+(1-y)\cdot\log(1-p)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Loss</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Loss}=-\left[y\cdot\log(p)+(1-y)\cdot\log(1-p)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the true label and <math id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted probability for class 1, i.e. the toxic class. The lower the value, the better the model’s performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Accuracy.</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fraction of labels correctly predicted by the model.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Step 1. Identifying Crucial Heads Using Activation Patching</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Activation patching, involves replacing the activations of a component of the model with those generated by a “corrupted prompt” and comparing the difference in performance. Some works replace the activations with zeros <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Olsson et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, while others replace it with the mean activation to eliminate all information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2022a</a>)</cite>. We use the former method based on activations with zeros. This activation patching is sequentially performed on all attention heads of the model and if patching a particular head drastically decreases the model’s performance, then that head is significantly contributing to the task and it is a <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crucial</span> head.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="Sx3.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12672v1/regular_accuracy_by_head.png" id="Sx3.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/323;" width="698" height="323" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Regular input.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="Sx3.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12672v1/adversarial_accuracy_by_head.png" id="Sx3.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/323;" width="698" height="323" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Adversarial input.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Change in accuracy by attention head on <span id="Sx3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">regular</span> and <span id="Sx3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">adversarial</span> input of the Jigsaw dataset. Cooler colors indicate a <span id="Sx3.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">decrease</span> in accuracy and warmer colors indicate an <span id="Sx3.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">increase</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Step 2. Adversarial Attacks</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once we have identified the relevant circuits, we use adversarial attacks on the model by modifying the inputs and observing these circuits. We aim to isolate components that activate in response to specific types of attacks or aspects of the input such as specific minority groups or languages.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">PGD BERT-Attack</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Waghela, Sen, and Rakshit 2024</a>)</cite>. This is a white-box adversarial attack that generates perturbations in the embedding space using Projected Gradient Descent (PGD). It first computes the gradient of the loss with respect to the input embeddings and iteratively applies small changes using another BERT model to maximize the loss, while keeping the perturbations within a bounded norm ball. These modified embeddings are then mapped back to the closest valid tokens for replacement. Figure <a href="#Sx3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Methodology ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is a depiction of BERT-Attack, the attack that PGD BERT Attack is based on.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">PGD BERT Attack allows us to assess model robustness under the worst-case scenario, imperceptible embedding-level perturbations that maintain grammatical structure but exploit model vulnerabilities. Samples of this can be seen in Table <a href="#S0.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Step 3. Identifying Vulnerable Heads on Adversarial Input</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once we have an adversarial version of the dataset generated using PGD BERT-Attack, we attempt to identify attention heads responsible for adversarial vulnerability. We systematically perform the previously mentioned activation patching on attention heads, zeroing out the output of each attention head independently and measuring the change in loss and accuracy. In this adversarial input, heads whose ablation <span id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reduces</span> loss are considered <span id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vulnerable</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="Sx3.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12672v1/jigsaw_dem_acc_change.png" id="Sx3.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/371;" width="698" height="371" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Jigsaw dataset</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="Sx3.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12672v1/toxigen_dem_acc_change.png" id="Sx3.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/371;" width="698" height="371" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Toxigen dataset</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Accuracy on adversarial data by demographic group with and without ablation across two datasets.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Experimental Setup</h2>

<div id="Sx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments to evaluate whether interpretable circuit-level interventions can improve the adversarial robustness of toxicity classifiers. Our pipeline consists of model training, adversarial input generation, mechanistic analysis of internal components, and targeted suppression of vulnerable attention heads. We used 1 H100 GPU to perform all the experiments.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Model Training</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We trained a BERT classifier using each of our datasets under consideration. On the Jigsaw dataset, with 160k training samples and 63k test samples, we achieved an accuracy of 92.86%. On the Toxigen dataset, with 188k training samples and 63k test samples, we achieved an accuracy of 84.5%. In addition to the trained classifier, we also tested the RoBERTa classifier by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Logacheva et al. (2022)</a></cite> on Jigsaw which achieved an accuracy of 93.4%. We use these as our reference performance scores that we then use for comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Adversarial Attack Procedure</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the test subsets of both datasets, we generated corresponding adversarial samples using the PGD BERT Attack. This attack method successfully perturbs 53.5% of the Jigsaw dataset and 98.2% of the Toxigen dataset. Of these successful perturbations we selected the perturbed samples that had a <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> 0.95 cosine similarity with the original text. 54% of the successful Jigsaw samples and 19% of the successful Toxigen samples met this criterion.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Results</h2>

<div id="Sx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we refer to our evaluations (Figures <a href="#Sx3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Step 1. Identifying Crucial Heads Using Activation Patching ‣ Methodology ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <a href="#Sx3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Step 3. Identifying Vulnerable Heads on Adversarial Input ‣ Methodology ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#Sx5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Results ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and discuss the insights obtained from them. Figure <a href="#Sx3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Step 1. Identifying Crucial Heads Using Activation Patching ‣ Methodology ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the change in accuracy when each attention head is individually ablated to 0 for regular input and adversarial input. Figure <a href="#Sx5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Results ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the same information only on adversarial input but disaggregated by demographic group. Finally, Figure <a href="#Sx3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Step 3. Identifying Vulnerable Heads on Adversarial Input ‣ Methodology ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the accuracy for each demographic group, without ablation and with the best ablated head, on adversarial input.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="Sx5.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12672v1/groups_heatmaps.png" id="Sx5.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:698/1765;" width="698" height="1765" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span> Toxigen Dataset</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="Sx5.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12672v1/jigsaw_heatmap_dem.png" id="Sx5.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:698/1765;" width="698" height="1765" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Jigsaw Dataset</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Change in accuracy by attention head on adversarial inputs, disaggregated by demographic group, for two datasets. Warmer colors indicate higher increase in accuracy.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Adversarial attacks can drastically degrade the performance of toxicity classifiers.</h5>

<div id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using PGD BERT Attack, we reduced the classification accuracy of a BERT-based toxicity model from <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">92.85% to 78.55%</span> on the Jigsaw dataset and from <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">84.5% to 2.8%</span> on the ToxiGen dataset. The attack also reduced the accuracy of the RoBERTa classifier from <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">93.4%</span> to <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">46.5%</span>. These attacks introduce imperceptible perturbations in the token embeddings that preserve semantic meaning while deceiving the classifier. This highlights how subtle manipulations can allow toxic content to evade detection, undermining the reliability of current moderation systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Classifiers have disjoint crucial and vulnerable attention heads.</h5>

<div id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using activation patching, we identified <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crucial</span> and <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vulnerable</span> attention heads. <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L0H4</span> is <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">crucial</span> because when zeroed out it decreases accuracy on clean input by <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">24%</span>. On the other hand, <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L0H2</span> and <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L1H3</span> do not significantly contribute to classification on clean inputs but are active during adversarial misclassifications. Suppressing these heads, as can be seen in Figure 2b, led to an <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8–9% increase in accuracy</span> on adversarial inputs, with a comparatively small loss in accuracy on clean examples. This indicates that these heads form part of adversarial pathways that can be selectively disabled.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Adversarial vulnerabilities disproportionately affect certain demographic groups, amplifying existing fairness concerns.</h5>

<div id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From Figure <a href="#Sx3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Step 3. Identifying Vulnerable Heads on Adversarial Input ‣ Methodology ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> it is evident that zeroing the most adversarially sensitive head per demographic group showed wide variation in accuracy improvement: <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">51.35%</span> for ”women”, but only <span id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">29.83%</span> for ”muslim” on the Toxigen dataset. This suggests that the impact of adversarial attacks is not equitable, raising fairness implications. Addressing adversarial robustness must therefore account for demographic-level disparities. Furthermore, in Figure <a href="#Sx5.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ Results ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> we can see that the demographic bias is structurally encoded into the model. Through mechanistic interpretability we are able to trace the specific components that contribute to this bias and eventually, mitigate them.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Some attention heads are vulnerable to adversarial input only for certain demographic groups.</h5>

<div id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#Sx5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Results ‣ Towards Inclusive Toxic Content Moderation: Addressing Vulnerabilities to Adversarial Attacks in Toxicity Classifiers Tackling LLM-generated Content" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the change in accuracy on adversarial input on headwise ablation disaggregated by demographic group. We see that there are some layers and attention heads that are vulnerable for all demographic groups to varying degrees. For example, layers 0,3 on Toxigen and and layers 3-6 on Jigsaw. However, there are also some heads and layers that are specifically vulnerable for certain demographic groups. For the Toxigen dataset, most heads in layer 9 are only vulnerable for the “asian” and “mexican” demographics. This could imply that heads in this layer are specifically operating on information for these demographics. Similarly, layer 10 head 7 for the Jigsaw dataset is specifically vulnerable for the “bisexual” demographic. Thus, we can see that demographic bias is structurally encoded into the model. Through mechanistic interpretability we are able to trace the specific components that contribute to this bias and, eventually, mitigate them.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx2.SSSx1.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our findings demonstrate that mechanistic interpretability can improve the robustness and fairness of toxicity classifiers. Rather than relying solely on external detectors or adversarial training, we propose using model-internal signals to uncover and address failure modes. This proactive strategy enhances not only classification accuracy under attack but also trust in automated moderation systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Conclusion</h2>

<div id="Sx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we present a novel framework for improving the robustness of transformer-based toxicity classifiers against adversarially generated text, including content produced by Large Language Models (LLMs). Our approach moves beyond black-box defenses by leveraging mechanistic interpretability to identify attention heads that are either crucial to model performance or vulnerable to adversarial manipulation. Through extensive experiments on both human-written (Jigsaw) and machine-generated (ToxiGen) datasets, we demonstrate three key findings: (1) adversarial perturbations can severely degrade model performance, (2) crucial and vulnerable attention heads are disjoint, allowing us to suppress the latter without large losses in clean accuracy, and (3) vulnerabilities are not evenly distributed across demographic groups, revealing fairness concerns that must be addressed in robust content moderation systems. Together, these findings illustrate that mechanistic interpretability can play a central role in proactively hardening toxicity classifiers, improving both accuracy and fairness under attack.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our results have important implications for the future of automated moderation. Rather than relying solely on adversarial training or external detectors, which are reactive and brittle to unseen attacks, our framework provides a proactive path toward defense by identifying and intervening on the internal components most responsible for misclassification. This contributes to building more trustworthy, transparent, and equitable toxicity detection models, supporting safer online discourse even in the presence of adaptive adversaries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations and Future Work</h2>

<div id="Sx7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our findings are promising, some limitations must be acknowledged. First, our experiments were restricted to English-language datasets and primarily evaluated BERT and RoBERTa models. As a result, it remains unclear whether our conclusions generalize to multilingual settings or to newer LLM-based classifiers used in production. Additionally, activation patching and adversarial generation are computationally expensive, limiting the scalability of our approach for very large models and real-time moderation. These limitations motivate several directions for future work:</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx7.SSx2.SSSx1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Runtime attack detection and suppression</h5>

<div id="Sx7.SSx2.SSSx1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.SSx2.SSSx1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We aim to move from offline mitigation to online defense, we have seen in Figure 2 that the <span id="Sx7.SSx2.SSSx1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vulnerable</span> attention heads still have some contribution to clean input accuracy. Indiscriminately setting them to zero is not the most efficient method of adversarial attack suppression. By monitoring the activations of known vulnerable heads during inference, we can proactively detect potential adversarial inputs. If such heads are triggered, their activations can be suppressed or replaced with robust alternatives. Further, the flagged inputs can be stored and used to build an adversarial training dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx7.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Identifying circuits containing multiple attention heads across layers</h5>

<div id="Sx7.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.SSx2.SSSx1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">So far we have only looked at the impact of single attention heads. From these experiments we observed that the highest impact was from heads in the first 2-3 layers. However, interpretability research has shown that heads in different layers often compose to form complex circuits that perform higher order thinking than a single head is capable of. We want to identify such pairs or triples and test whether suppressing them together further improves attack resistance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx7.SSx2.SSSx1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Extension to Multilingual and Minority Group-Specific Classifiers</h5>

<div id="Sx7.SSx2.SSSx1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.SSx2.SSSx1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the disparities in adversarial vulnerabilities across demographics, future work will also focus on understanding how linguistic and cultural structures influence model robustness, especially in low-resource languages or for underrepresented groups.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
